  "answer": "### Issue Presented\nThe income tax return filing deadline is not the same as the prescriptive period for assessment. Under Section 203 of the National Internal Revenue Code (NIRC), the prescriptive period for the assessment of internal revenue taxes is three years from the last day prescribed for filing the return. If a return is filed late, the three-year period is counted from the date the return was actually filed.\n\n**Legal Basis:**\n- **NIRC Section 203**: \"Except as provided in Section 222, internal revenue taxes shall be assessed within three (3) years after the last day prescribed by law for the filing of the return...\"\n\nThis means that while the filing deadline is a specific date set by the BIR (usually April 15 for individual income tax returns), the prescriptive period for assessment extends for three years from that deadline, allowing the BIR to assess taxes based on the filed return.\n\n### Relevant Facts / Assumptions\nThe factual circumstances have not been fully described. The analysis is based on the question as presented.\n\n### Alternative Interpretations\nNo alternative interpretations identified from the available indexed authorities.\n\n### Position Strength\nPosition strength is preliminary and subject to full fact pattern verification.",
